          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "At what blood pressure reading is hypertension diagnosed, and do the major bodies agree?",
          "answer": "Hypertension is diagnosed when blood pressure is persistently elevated over time, rather than based on a single reading (Major cardiology society consensus guidance). A diagnosis typically requires an average of two or more blood pressure readings taken on two or more separate occasions (Major cardiology society consensus guidance). Out-of-office measurements, such as home readings or 24-hour monitoring, are used to confirm the diagnosis (Major cardiology society consensus guidance).\n\nMajor bodies do differ on the exact blood pressure reading that defines hypertension (Major cardiology society consensus guidance). Guidance in the United States sets the diagnostic threshold for hypertension at 130/80 mm Hg (guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets). In contrast, European guidance defines hypertension as beginning at 140/90 mm Hg (guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets).\n\nTherefore, the same blood pressure reading may be categorized differently depending on which guidance is applied (Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets). For instance, United States guidance considers lower thresholds as \"stage 1\" hypertension, where lifestyle changes alone might be advised for some individuals (Major cardiology society consensus guidance). European guidance, however, considers readings between 130-139 / 80-89 mm Hg as high-normal blood pressure rather than Stage 1 hypertension (guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
